{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1198572-radically-changing-secondary-creature-types","id":1198572,"title":"Radically changing secondary creature types","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-03-22T23:26:16-07:00","id":1198572,"title":"Radically changing secondary creature types","content":"I was thinking about this while answering a crossover based question about werewolves in a Mage game.<br /> <br /> Often there are games with limited crossover, that is to say that some creature from another game line is used as NPC's for your primary game. Vampire villains in a Werewolf game, an active Werewolf cairn in your Mage game, a Giovanni vampire who summons wraiths, and so on.<br /> <br /> Well, I have seen this done from time to time and unfortunately, there is always a tendency for players to fall back on their out of game knowledge. Werewolf players are sure that fighting a vampire will be a cakewalk, mages about to fight werewolves turn all of their bullets silver, and so on. Even if the planning isn't quite so obvious, the players know what to expect and so they aren't experiencing the sort of nervousness that their characters should.<br /> <br /> So I think that's it's a good idea to change things up a bit on players. So that those vampires/werewolves/mages/wraiths/fairies aren't what you are (at least on some level) expecting.<br /> <br /> This also allows changes that can provide better balance, thematic fit, or cosmological consistency. For example, if vampires are going to be enemies in a werewolf game, make them fomori. Why not, babes could conceptually fit with vampires and they fit with werewolf too. In the other hand, maybe werewolves in your Vampire game can only turn into wolves (no Crinos) but don't take aggravated damage from silver and have an entirely different ideology that doesn't require that they kill on sight.<br /> <br /> I've only tried this a few times over the years, largely because I haven't run many crossovers. Has anybody else done this and if so, how has it work? Have players been irritated at the change?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1129,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1129-sergeant-brother","user":"Sergeant Brother","date":". 03-24-2018, 08:29 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1129-sergeant-brother","name":"Sergeant Brother","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1129_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-24T04:42:49-07:00","id":1199003,"title":"","content":"I've done it in Mage, repeatedly. I think in mage it is easiest, because the \u201cvampire\u201c could be a spirit, a mage (marauder/nephandus), a hobgoblin, quiet or the players in question could be in a realm.<br /> <br /> Sometimes the least expected possibility was in fact the vampire being a vampire...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>So, this Zen Master walks up to a hot dog stand and says: &quot;Make me one with everything!&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12022-nonsense","name":"Nonsense","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12022_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"815"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-24T17:27:50-07:00","id":1199263,"title":"","content":"When ever I've run Werewolf, I've tended to make vampires Fomori, mages use all Gifts and Rites, and ghosts made using spirit rules, all for simplicity's sake. <br /> <br /> The rules for the &quot;demon-eaten&quot; in the revised Book of Madness for Mage were deliberately designed so that you could make basic vampires and werewolves with them, and I've used them for such.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-24T22:38:14-07:00","id":1199306,"title":"","content":"Generally I agree with the sentiment but one of the specifics really stuck out, the werewolf example.. I mean even without metagaming that's what most Western educated people would probably do if they heard werewolf. Seems rather reasonable.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}